WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.680
Meet your new Princess Peach! The&nbsp;
Official Trailer for "The Super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.000
Mario Bros. Movie" is finally here,&nbsp;
and on top of revealing the Princess's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.620
new voice, it's full of fun "Mario"&nbsp;
references, both obvious and obscure.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.560
To kick off the second "Super Mario Bros. Movie"&nbsp;
trailer in grand fashion, we see Mario emerge from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.720
a dark tunnel into a gladiatorial arena to face&nbsp;
his opponent – Donkey Kong. The two put on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.760
game faces and charge toward one another as we get&nbsp;
a better look at the massive steel beam structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.080
they're standing on. While it may be odd to&nbsp;
see Mario and DK as adversaries these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.360
the two gaming icons started out as opponents,&nbsp;
and in a similar environment, to boot.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.060
Way back in 1981, Nintendo released the "Donkey&nbsp;
Kong" arcade game. It has players control a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:49.620
little mustachioed man who later became Mario&nbsp;
as he attempts to rescue his beloved Pauline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.980
who has been captured by the&nbsp;
titular gorilla. To save her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.360
Mario must scale level after level of&nbsp;
steel beams – much like the ones in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.982
trailer – while dodging barrels and defending&nbsp;
himself against Kong's other forms of attack.

00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:06.443
"Donkey Kong! Why would you do that!?"

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.540
In 1994, Nintendo brought Donkey Kong back in a&nbsp;
big way with "Donkey Kong Country," which actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.400
starred the grandson of the original Kong. The&nbsp;
original, now-elderly arcade star appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:23.280
"Donkey Kong Country" as Cranky Kong, aka Donkey&nbsp;
Kong Sr. In the trailer, an ape who looks a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.604
like Cranky is watching the battle, clearly&nbsp;
happy to see the young gorilla take Mario down.

00:01:28.604 --> 00:01:32.400
All in all, the newest "Super Mario&nbsp;
Bros. Movie" trailer is a fast-paced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.340
blink-and-you'll-miss-it affair&nbsp;
that serves as a highlight reel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.760
for the film itself. That means that it&nbsp;
doesn't give viewers much time to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.180
in the animated environments that the&nbsp;
movie's heroes and villains inhabit.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.360
Nevertheless, we do get a look&nbsp;
at some noteworthy locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.380
throughout that fans of the "Super Mario"&nbsp;
franchise are quite familiar with. First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.800
and foremost are good looks at the&nbsp;
Mushroom Kingdom and Bowser's lair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.160
but they're just the tip of the iceberg&nbsp;
when it comes to famous locales.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.560
Thanks to a map that several Toads examine, we&nbsp;
see that areas like "Shifting Sand Land" exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.000
in this version of the "Super Mario" universe.&nbsp;
We also see the home of some Cheep Cheeps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.581
who jump effortlessly over bridges just as&nbsp;
they do in their video game appearances.

00:02:10.581 --> 00:02:14.859
"Ah! Get it off get it off get it off!"

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:19.680
Speaking of homes, the trailer also offers a&nbsp;
brief look at Yoshi's Island, where several Yoshis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.140
run from an unseen entity. Finally, there's the&nbsp;
notorious "Mario Kart" track, Rainbow Road, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:30.840
our heroes bravely drive on, despite the lack of&nbsp;
guardrails — the bane of many a gamer's existence.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.120
One of the many selling points of the new "Super&nbsp;
Mario Bros. Movie" teaser is the introduction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.040
Anya Taylor-Joy's take on Princess Peach. She's&nbsp;
noticeably absent from the previous trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.120
so she gets a lot of time to shine&nbsp;
in this one. Not only does it appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.480
as though she won't be kidnapped by&nbsp;
Bowser, but she seems to be a strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.400
capable leader. Peach makes no bones about her&nbsp;
desire to take the fight to Bowser if it means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.900
keeping her kingdom safe, and in doing so,&nbsp;
the trailer reveals that she'll wear a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.200
different outfits that have histories&nbsp;
rooted in the "Super Mario" franchise.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.520
It should come as no surprise that, for much of&nbsp;
the second "Super Mario Bros. Movie" trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.040
Princess Peach is wearing her most recognizable&nbsp;
costume: a pink dress, golden crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.240
and white gloves. However, it does change&nbsp;
when she picks up the flame of a Fire Flower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.620
with her attire taking on a white and red&nbsp;
motif, a mechanic that countless "Super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:23.820
Mario" games have employed over the years.&nbsp;
Departing far from her traditional look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.260
Peach is also shown wearing a white and&nbsp;
pink jumpsuit with a pink scarf. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.387
look is practically a direct translation&nbsp;
of her "Mario Kart 8" racing costume.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.620
The second "Super Mario Bros. Movie" trailer is&nbsp;
worth skimming through for costume changes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.180
famous locations galore, but one would be remiss&nbsp;
not to bring up the countless beloved characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.220
that make split-second cameos throughout. In&nbsp;
Bowser's lair alone, we see a few Bob-ombs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:52.980
Shy Guys, Goombas, Hammer Bros., and Piranha&nbsp;
Plants among a large crowd of Koopa Troopas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:58.080
A Bullet Bill later pops up to give Mario a hard&nbsp;
time, much like the aforementioned Cheep Cheeps.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.580
For those hoping to see more familiar faces than&nbsp;
those already announced, "The Super Mario Bros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.500
Movie" more than has you covered. For all of you&nbsp;
Kong family fans out there, you'll be delighted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:12.120
know that several Kongs pop up in this teaser.&nbsp;
In addition to Donkey Kong and Cranky Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.860
you can also see several other Kongs like&nbsp;
Diddy and Funky as well. The Rainbow Road&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.750
shots toward the end of the trailer are full of&nbsp;
characters riding along in their outlandish karts.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.000
When one thinks of the "Super Mario" franchise,&nbsp;
a handful of things likely spring to mind. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.500
characters and landscapes are likely up there, as&nbsp;
well as the sounds and music that accompany them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:36.300
When it comes to visual elements, Warp Pipes are&nbsp;
almost certainly among the first things to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.740
up. After all, they've been a part of Mario's&nbsp;
video game story from the very beginning. Mario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:46.320
uses them to "warp" around his many quests and&nbsp;
even access secret rooms. Therefore, it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.880
sense that they'd appear in the upcoming film,&nbsp;
and we might know what kind of purpose they serve.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.020
It's heavily implied in both "Super&nbsp;
Mario Bros. Movie" trailers so far that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.058
Mario and Luigi aren't from the Mushroom&nbsp;
Kingdom or any of the surrounding areas.

00:04:59.333 --> 00:05:03.953
"There's a human, has a mustache, just like you."

00:05:03.953 --> 00:05:09.060
Especially in Mario's case, he seems lost and&nbsp;
pretty overwhelmed by the situation he's in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.040
The new teaser further fleshes out this idea&nbsp;
by including a few shots of Mario and Luigi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.720
tumbling through mysterious, winding lanes&nbsp;
that weave in and out of clouds. These paths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.700
lead to green ports that evoke images of Warp&nbsp;
Pipes. Could these Warp Pipes be portals to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:28.163
other worlds that Mario and Luigi plunge into?&nbsp;
We'll have to wait and see to know for sure.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.900
Standing side-by-side with Princess Peach in&nbsp;
her defense of the Mushroom Kingdom is Toad. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.720
short, mushroom-headed adventurer is a video game&nbsp;
legend who made his debut in 1985's "Super Mario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.880
Bros." In the decades since, he's become a fan&nbsp;
favorite the world over, and his appearance has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.520
become iconic. For "The Super Mario Bros. Movie,"&nbsp;
he seems to maintain his usual ensemble of a blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:55.860
and yellow vest with white pants and a red and&nbsp;
white mushroom cap, which is definitively not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:00.660
a hat. However, he also seems to pay homage&nbsp;
to another, less well-known Toad character.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.040
As Toad and Peach go out to confront an&nbsp;
invading Bowser, we see them each brandish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.080
a weapon of some kind. Peach goes for a spear&nbsp;
with two ax blades attached to the top, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.240
Toad grabs something a bit less intimidating:&nbsp;
a frying pan. This is far from an ideal weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.800
for battling someone as formidable as Bowser,&nbsp;
but it is a fun reference to Super Nintendo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.720
World favorite Chef Toad. Introduced in 2021&nbsp;
with the opening of Super Nintendo World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.700
the theme park character is the head chef at&nbsp;
Kinopio's Café. In most depictions, he's shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.260
holding a frying pan, hence the connection to&nbsp;
the "Super Mario Bros. Movie" version of Toad.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.180
But that's not the only Toad that this version&nbsp;
of the character is inspired by. In 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.400
Nintendo released a spin-off game for its popular&nbsp;
"Super Mario 3D World" platformer. The new game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.360
"Captain Toad Treasure Tracker" featured&nbsp;
a unique version of the Toad character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:53.460
carrying a very heavy explorer's backpack.&nbsp;
Toad in the "Super Mario Bros. Movie" dons a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.215
familiar accessory on his back as he heads&nbsp;
out for adventure with Peach and Mario.

